course: religion-ethics
lesson: 92
title: Cheating at school: copying and peeking
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Cheating at school means getting a mark for work or answers that are not really yours: copying, peeking, using hidden notes or handing in someone else's work.
explain: Mason says, "Lemme copy your maths, I'll change a few answers so it's not sus"; but homework is practice, so copying skips the learning, and the teacher thinks he understands when he doesn't.
explain: The cheat loses most: "Food gained by fraud tastes sweet, but one ends up with a mouth full of gravel" (Proverbs 20:17).
explain: Small cheating trains the habit, and honest work trains honesty: "Whoever can be trusted with very little can also be trusted with much" (Luke 16:10).
explain: If you are tempted because you're scared or stuck, tell the teacher; you'll get help, not trouble.
fact: Copying homework means you skip the learning, and the teacher thinks you understand when you don't.
fact: "Food gained by fraud tastes sweet, but one ends up with a mouth full of gravel" (Proverbs 20:17).
fact: A peek is cheating even if you'd have got most of it right; if everyone peeked, tests would mean nothing.
fact: Handing in AI-written work as yours is like copying; schools have their own rules about how AI may be used.
fact: A prize for work a parent did is unfair to the other children: "The Lord detests dishonest scales" (Proverbs 11:1).
fact: Fear and pressure make cheating more likely; "Cast all your anxiety on him" (1 Peter 5:7), and tell your teacher you're struggling.
fact: "Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong" (Exodus 23:2): if lots of kids cheat, you're not a fool for being honest.
fact: Letting someone copy is cheating too; Paul says not to "put any stumbling block or obstacle in the way of a brother or sister" (Romans 14:13).
fact: Self-marking is an honour system: it only works with trust; an honest 8 out of 10 beats a fake 10.
fact: "A man reaps what he sows" (Galatians 6:7): skipped reading leaves gaps you trip over later.
fact: God sees what no one else sees (Psalm 139:1-4); the vicar calls honesty "what we are when only God is watching".
fact: Plato's story of the ring of Gyges, a ring that made its wearer invisible, asks whether you would still be honest if nobody could see you.
fact: "Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart" (Colossians 3:23).
fact: Slang check: "sus" means suspicious; "bare man" (London) means loads of people; "a mug" means a fool; "lowkey" means sort of.
exercise: Mason wants to copy your maths and change a few answers "so it's not sus". Who gets hurt most? | Mason, because he skips the learning | Nobody, because homework doesn't count | Only the teacher, who has to mark it | Only you, because your answers are shared
exercise: What can you offer Mason instead? | To explain the maths so he can do it | To do his homework for him next time | To tell the whole class he copies | Nothing, he should work it out alone
exercise: Ellie can see Josh's paper and is unsure of one word. Is a peek cheating? | Yes; the mark only means something if it's hers | No; she knew all the other words anyway, so it's fair | No; one word is far too small to count as cheating | Yes, but only if Josh got that word right himself
exercise: Riley's cousin says, "Just get the AI to write your story." What is the honest view? | Check the school rule; handing in AI work as yours is dishonest | AI stories are always fine because computers aren't people | It's fine as long as you change a few words | It's only cheating if the AI makes mistakes
exercise: Zara's mum made most of her science project, and Zara won. What should Zara do? | Tell the teacher honestly how much Mum did | Keep the prize; it's her name on it, so it's her win | Give the prize to her mum instead | Say nothing and win next year too
exercise: Oliver writes spelling words on his hand because he's scared. What should he do? | Wash it off and tell the teacher he's struggling | Write them smaller next time, so nobody can see them | Write them on his ruler instead, where it's less obvious | Stay at home on test days, so he never has to sit them
exercise: Tyrese says, "Bare man got the answers, you're a mug if you don't." What does "a mug" mean? | A fool | A cup | A friend | A teacher
exercise: If lots of kids cheat, are you a fool for being honest? | No; your mark is real, and you can quietly tell the teacher | Yes; honest kids always lose out while the cheats get top marks | Yes; you should cheat to keep up | No, but only if you get top marks
exercise: Ollie let his friend copy, and both got caught. Is letting someone copy cheating? | Yes; both fool the teacher | No; only the copier is cheating | No; it's just being a good friend | Yes, but only if the friend gets a better mark
exercise: Lily changes one wrong answer when the class marks its own tests. What is that? | Cheating; an honest 8 beats a fake 10 | Fine, because she's marking her own | Fine, because it's only one word | Smart, because the teacher trusts them
exercise: Ethan says he read the whole book but skipped half. Who loses most? | Ethan, because he misses the reading he needs | The teacher, who is a bit fooled and has to check every book again | The author, who doesn't get paid | Nobody, reading levels don't matter
exercise: Jaden says copying's "lowkey fine if it's just homework". What is wrong? | Small cheating trains the habit; homework is practice for honesty | Nothing; homework really doesn't count | Copying is only wrong in real tests | It's fine if you copy from a clever kid
exercise: The teacher leaves the answer sheet on her desk. "Quick, look!" What should you do? | Don't look; turn it over and tell her when she's back | Look quickly and change just a few answers, so it's not obvious | Take a photo for later | Let a friend look but not yourself
exercise: What does Proverbs 20:17 compare cheating to? | Sweet food that turns to gravel in your mouth | A tree that grows tall and strong beside the water | A river that flows on and on until it reaches the sea | A house built on rock that stands firm in the storm
exercise: What does the vicar call honesty? | What we are when only God is watching | What we do when the teacher is looking | What we say at church on Sundays | What we promise in a test
test: Your mate says, "Copying homework helps you, you get a better mark." What actually happens? | You skip the learning and get stuck in the real test | You learn just as much as doing it yourself, only faster | Teachers give extra marks for copying | Your brain learns from looking at answers
test: "Bro, everyone's using the AI, don't be a mug," says Riley's cousin. What is the wise step? | Check the school rule, and don't hand in work that isn't yours | Use it and change one word per sentence | Use it, because everyone else is, and the teacher will never know | Ask the AI if it's cheating
test: A cartoon shows a kid who hides answers in his shoe, gets 10 out of 10 and lives happily ever after. What is wrong? | He learned nothing, and gaps trip you up later | Nothing; that's how school works, and he got full marks | He should have hidden them in his hat | Teachers never check shoes
test: "Shut up and let me copy, you're meant to be my mate," says Mason. What is the best reply? | I'll explain it, but I won't let you copy | Fine, take it, mates do anything for each other | Get lost, I'm telling Miss right now | Only if you give me your crisps
test: Zara's friend says, "Your mum did your project? Same as everyone, chill." What is the honest view? | Help is fine, but a prize for Mum's work is unfair to other kids | It's fine because all parents help loads | The prize is fair because Zara carried it in | It's only unfair if Mum wins next year too
test: What would happen if everyone peeked in every test? | Tests would mean nothing and nobody would know who needs help | Everyone would learn faster and better, and get top marks every time | Teachers would give everyone a prize | Nothing; tests don't matter anyway
test: Oliver whispers, "I wrote them on my hand 'cause I'm scared I'll fail." What helps him most? | Telling his teacher he's struggling so he gets help | Writing them in smaller letters, so the teacher won't see | Sitting right at the back of the class, where nobody looks | Never doing spelling tests again, if his mum writes a note
test: Lily says, "It's my own test, so changing it isn't cheating." What is wrong? | Self-marking is a trust system; changing the mark fools the teacher | Nothing; she can mark herself however she likes | She should change two answers to be fair | It's only cheating if she gets caught
test: Jaden in Atlanta: "Homework's lowkey nothing, so who cares?" What does Luke 16:10 suggest? | Trust in small things leads to trust in big things | Small things never matter to anybody, only the big ones do | Only big tests count in life | Homework is for teachers, not pupils
test: The answer sheet is on Miss's desk and nobody's watching. Which story fits this test of honesty? | Plato's ring of Gyges: honesty when no one can see | Goldilocks, who ate the bears' porridge while they were out | Jack and the beanstalk, where Jack climbs up to the giant | The gingerbread man, who runs away from everyone he meets
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the principal harm occasioned by the copying of homework? | The copier is deprived of the practice for which homework is intended | The teacher is deprived of an opportunity to mark the work | The pupil whose work is copied is invariably penalised alone | No harm is occasioned, homework being of no consequence
exam: Given that the pupils of a class are permitted to mark their own tests, identify the statement most consistent with this lesson. | The arrangement depends upon trust, and the alteration of one's own mark constitutes cheating | The arrangement permits each pupil to award whatever mark he prefers | The arrangement renders cheating impossible | The arrangement is unaffected by the honesty of the pupils
exam: Select the response consistent with the teaching of Exodus 23:2 where many pupils obtain test answers improperly. | The pupil ought not to follow the majority in wrongdoing | The pupil ought to follow the majority in order to remain competitive | The pupil ought to obtain the answers from a different source | The pupil ought to absent himself from the test
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate course of action for a pupil who is tempted to cheat by reason of fear. | The communication of the difficulty to the teacher, with a view to obtaining assistance | The concealment of notes upon the person in a less visible location | The deliberate absence from the test on a pretext | The copying of answers from a neighbouring pupil
